<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willibald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700–<a href="/years/787">787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Willibald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willibald was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> monk and pilgrim, a kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>. He travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> in the 720s, the first recorded English pilgrim there, and spent ten years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>. Boniface made him the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> around 741.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willibald.jpg" alt="Willibald" width="320" height="751" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rensi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2010_Willibald_Handschuher-Figur_Spitalkirche_Eichst%C3%A4tt.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Willibald was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> on 21 October around the year 700. His father was Richard the Pilgrim, a chieftain of Wessex, and his mother Wuna of Wessex, reportedly a sister of Boniface; his brother was Winibald and his sister Walburga. After he survived a violent illness at the age of three, his parents kept their vow to give him to the monastic life, and at five he entered the Benedictine monastery at Waldheim under Abbot Egwald. Most of what is known of him comes from the Hodoeporicon, an account of his journeys that he dictated shortly before his death to Huneberc, a nun of Heidenheim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 721 Willibald set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with his father and brother. Richard died at Lucca, and the brothers went on to Rome, where both fell gravely ill; Huneberc calls the sickness plague, though one later writer thought it malaria. Willibald left Rome in 724 and sailed by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, visiting the tomb of John the Evangelist at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, then travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 11 November 725 and visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, Gaza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and many other holy places.</p><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he lived for about two years in a room beside the Church of the Holy Apostles, and then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. With his companion Tidbercht he joined the community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>, where he served as sacrist, dean and porter. In 738 Boniface asked Pope Gregory III to send Willibald to help in his German missions. At Eichstätt Boniface ordained him priest on 22 July 741. In 742 he and Winibald founded the double monastery of Heidenheim, where Winibald was the first abbot and Walburga later the first abbess.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Willibald led the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> for more than four decades, living in its monastery, which became an important centre for the spread of monasticism in the region; how many he converted is not known. He is the first known Englishman to have visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. His body and relics from his journeys are kept in his shrine at Eichstätt Cathedral, and his feast day is 7 July.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Willibald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saint Boniface made his kinsman <b>Willibald</b> the first bishop of Eichstätt in the 740s.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-eichstatt">Prince-Bishopric of Eichstätt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.89,11.18&amp;year=756" aria-label="Eichstätt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Willibald"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.89,11.18&amp;year=756">What was here in 756 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
